'''Myocardial infarcton'''
The cardiac muscle cells die due to the lack of nutrients especially oxygen, this results in poor blood flow to the myocardium due to the narrowing or complete occlusion of one or more coronary arteries. The infarction will depend on the collateral flow, the metabolic requirements of the cell and the duriation of the insult. The main cause of myocardial infarction is of '''Atheroma''' of the coronary vessels.
'''Angina''' occurs due to the plaque ('''stable plaque''') which narrows the '''coronary arteries''', causing the myocardical cells to become ischaemic, the damage caused is reversible.
'''Unstable plaques''' may not produce any clinical problems until an acute even occurs when the fibrous cap of the plaque splits and blood from the lumen can reach the soft necrotic centre.